<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hürrem Sultan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1505–<a href="/years/1558">1558</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Roxelana, Haseki Hürrem Sultan · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-dynasty">Ottoman dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hürrem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a>, known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> as Roxelana, was the chief consort and legal wife of the Ottoman sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent">Suleiman the Magnificent</a>, the mother of his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-ii">Selim II</a> and the first haseki sultan. Presumably born in Ruthenia to an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> family, she was captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-tatars">Crimean Tatars</a> in a slave raid, taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and became Suleiman's favourite concubine around his accession in 1520. Breaking with custom, he freed and married her, and she became his most trusted adviser, influencing appointments and diplomacy, the first in a series of powerful women of the Sultanate of Women. She founded the Haseki Sultan Complex and died in 1558.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hurrem-sultan.jpg" alt="Hürrem Sultan" width="330" height="422" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Workshop of Titian · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Titian_-_Portrait_of_a_Woman_(La_Sultana_Rossa).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>No document records Hürrem's life before she entered the harem, and her birth name is unknown. Legends of her origins arose in literature, and she has been claimed as Ukrainian, Russian or Polish. Many sources place her in Ruthenia, then part of the Polish Crown. The ambassador Ogier Ghiselin de Busbecq, in his Turkish Letters, published in 1581, called her Roxelana after Roxolania, the name often given to that territory. According to accounts, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-tatars">Crimean Tatars</a> kidnapped her in a slave raid between 1512 and 1520, and she may have been taken first to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Kaffa</a>, a major centre of the Ottoman slave trade, before reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Her rise from slave to legal wife attracted jealousy in the harem and among the general population. No law forbade the marriage, but custom was strong and astonished the court. The wedding probably took place around 1533. She bore at least six children, five of them sons, which broke the old custom that a concubine gave the sultan only one son. Her daughter Mihrimah married Rüstem Pasha, later grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>, on 26 November 1539.</p><p>Suleiman spent nearly ten years of his reign on campaign, and from the 1530s Hürrem wrote to him about the capital and the statesmen. She is known as the first woman in Ottoman history to concern herself with state affairs. Two of her letters to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-ii-augustus">Sigismund II Augustus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> survive, one congratulating him on his accession in 1548, and during her lifetime the empire's relations with Poland were generally peaceful. Her patronage of public buildings extended to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, though the full list of works is unknown.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Hürrem died on 15 April 1558 of an unknown illness. The French ambassador Jean de la Vigne reported that Suleiman's grief was so great that he seemed to have aged. Her funeral prayers were led by the chief mufti Ebu Suud, and she was buried in a domed mausoleum decorated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Iznik</a> tiles beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleymaniye-mosque">Süleymaniye Mosque</a>, where Suleiman was laid eight years later. Her son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-ii">Selim II</a> ascended the throne on 30 September 1566. European artists, barred from the harem, created a largely imagined image of her. The French author Gabriel Bounin wrote the tragedy La Soltane in 1561, and she has since inspired paintings, Haydn's Symphony No. 63, plays and novels.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hürrem Sultan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In reality the planning began before 1550 and parts of the complex were not completed until after 1557: the final construction expenses were recorded in 1559, relating to some of the madrasas and to the mausoleum of <b>Hürrem Sultan</b> (d. 1558).</blockquote><a href="/work/suleymaniye-mosque">Süleymaniye Mosque · How It Was Made</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Rohatyn, in what is now <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/topkapi-palace">Topkapı Palace</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.42,24.62&amp;year=1539" aria-label="Rohatyn on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Hürrem Sultan"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4701 5888h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4701 5888h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.42,24.62&amp;year=1539">What was here in 1539? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
